Hello everyone, my name is Sujal, and today I will present our Week 3 progress update for our game called Everyday Dilemma.
Our game is designed for university students aged 18 to 25 years old to help them learn about cyber-safety through interactive decision-making.
The current prototype includes an animated welcome screen and three realistic scam scenarios that students may face online.
These scenarios include a fake homework re-upload email scam, a fake IT support pop-up asking for login details, and an urgent bank alert text message scam.
This week, we improved the structure of our project by separating the layout, design, and game logic into organized files for easier development.
We also added educational feedback that explains why an answer is correct or unsafe, along with a live scoreboard to track player progress.
Our testing results showed that the main interactions, gameplay choices, and score counters are all working successfully.
However, feedback from other groups helped us identify a text wrapping issue that affects the display on smaller mobile screens.
All of our work and evidence have been uploaded to GitHub under the folders Week-3-Development-Testing, Week-3-Appendix-Planning, and Peer-Feedback.
Before Week 4, our team plans to fix the mobile display issue, add more cyber-safety challenges, and continue preparing for our final presentation together.
